  Problem 6a) ""So you'd like to know how to log in to stokes at UCF through terminal? well look no further as you have found the perfect in depth description and instructions for just that. First dear reader ensure you are connected to the UCF vpn using whichever method you may favor(I use the Cisco Secure Client application), after being being prompted and successfully logging in please navigate to your terminal and type the following command in 'ssh NID@stokes.ist.ucf.edu' where the NID is your UCF NID. If successful you should be prompted to enter your password and then possibly for authentication. If you have completed all these steps correctly then CONGRATULATIONS you have officially logged into Stokes for the university of central florida, you can confirm by looking at your user in the terminal, it should now be your UCF NID. If these instructions did not suffice may I offer both my sincerest apologies and condolences as well as these official instructions by the university themselves. (https://arcc.ist.ucf.edu/docs/access/linux/). PLEASE NOTE: these instructions are for how to log into stokes specifically for Linux/Mac/Unix. Thank you so much and Godspeed reader.""
  Problem 6b) And we are not yet finished, it appears this is going to be commonplace. Now I will provide in depth instructions and an explanation for how I created a homework 2 folder within stokes in terminal. Please find below
  ""Reader! you're back! and what's that this time you'd like to know how to create a homework 2 folder in stokes in your terminal? Well it is your lucky day. If you followed the previous instructions for logging into stokes successfully(problem 6a) then you should now be within Stokes in your terminal. The command for creating a new folder(or directory) is 'mkdir' so you will type that exact command into your terminal and don't forget to include the name of the file, in this specific instance for me it will be hw2_username, where username is my(or your) username. You can then check to see if you did this successfully by typing the command 'ls' this will list all the files(or directories) within your current working or home directory, if you see the file there then congratulations you have done it, now we will move on to the next step in the next problem.
  Problem 6c) WELCOME BACK to part 3 in this multi-part series in which I explain my homework assignment procedure to you. In this episode we are now asked to copy our previously created .py file(see problem 6 in this log entry for further details) into the new homework 2 folder we have created in stokes. reader, this is where it all went wrong. Dear reader, the command to copy the desired file on your local directory to the stokes directory is 'scp' it should be scp ~/file path/file/ username@stokes.ist.ucf.edu, in a perfect world this would go seamlessly. However, unfortunately, the world is not perfect and stokes is evil so it will not let me do this successfully(something about my password or verification being wrong, so strange seeing as I was able to log in originally but now something is not working). I hope you find more success than me, but this is where I must leave you to fly on your own as I figure this issue out(complain to the TA or the Stokes administration). 
